<td id="S4.T1.2.1.17.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.1.17.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8.9</span><span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                    
                    
                    
                    
                    
                    
                    
                  <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">On Qwen no fine-tune, we observe much lower performance compared to what is reported in the Qwen report </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite><span id="footnote2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">. The discrepancy is due to our (zero shot) prompting and the resulting CoT generation format, and does not affect our RL trained models (see Appendix </span><a href="#A9" title="Appendix I Format Following ‣ Soft Tokens, Hard Truths" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a><span id="footnote2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> for details).</span></span></span></span><math id="S4.T1.m127" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#F4EEFF;" mathbackground="#F4EEFF" mathsize="0.800em">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T1.2.1.17.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.0</span></td>
